+| Suite | Command | Host | What it reports |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| B1 | `python -m bench.b1_kernels` | laptop (+ server via `b2_server_point`) | kernel timings (K1 snapshot, GEMV, render with T_FD ∈ {0, 8, 16, 32}, noise) per backend; exponents vs O(L·N) and O(L·T·S); NNLS fit of the §7.4 page-cost model from engine runs |
+| B2 | `python -m bench.b2_lockstep` | laptop, server | lockstep RTF and RTF_min (100 ms windows) on one-factor slices S1–S5 of L × N × RIS count × f_s × F0..F5, plus S6 (numba thread scaling); non-zero RIS path asserted per scene; `dropped.csv` lists every point of the full product not run |
+| B3 | `python -m bench.b3_paced` | **Linux (AWS)**; laptop only as "Windows, not representative" | paced sustainable L per page target, miss rate at 0/1/5 ms slack, page-latency histogram (CCDF); `protocol_12_3` in the summary |
+| B4 | `python -m bench.b4_control --part lockstep` / `--part paced` | laptop / Linux | command lateness (lockstep must be 0) and visible latency, 10 Hz–10 kHz, in-process, remote barrier and paced async |
+| B5 | `python -m bench.b5_v1` | same host only | ANTS Fig. 3 workload: v1-as-committed (+ the one import fix) vs sremu |
+| B6 | `python -m bench.b6_crosstool` | laptop | time per RIS channel evaluation: sremu, MATLAB R2026a (vectorized and per-element loop, toolbox path, `RISReflector`), v2; literature numbers as REPORTED context |
+| E-B3 | `python -m bench.eb3_paced` | Linux (AWS) | external TDMA controller over ZMQ, paced + async: lateness and switch-inside-packet rate (not a PER) |
+
+`sremu bench --suite {b1,…,b6,eb3} [args]` runs the same scripts. `python -m bench.run_laptop`
+runs the laptop campaign (B1, B2, B4 lockstep, B5, B6, B3 "Windows") with load gates, and
+`python -m bench.summarize` collects the headline numbers of the latest run of every suite
+into `runs/bench/bench_summary.json`.
+
+Every suite writes one run directory `runs/bench/<UTC>_<suite>[_<tag>]/` with
+`manifest.json` (git commit and dirty flag, versions, platform, power plan or governor,
+threads, host-load samples), `spec.json` (hashed into `scenario_sha256`), CSV/JSON data,
+`scenarios/*.yaml` (every resolved scenario, hashed in the tables), `meta.json` (data
+classes: the timings are `MEAS` on the stated host, `workload_data_classes` says what was
+timed; platform label, caveats, background-load summary, quiet-gate result,
+`interrupted`) and `summary.json`. Numbers are reported exactly as measured; nothing is
+compared across machines.
+
+**Shared host.** numba's OpenMP kernels slow down by orders of magnitude when other
+processes occupy CPUs (every parallel region waits for its slowest thread). The suites
+therefore record the background busy fraction (system busy minus the suite's own CPU
+share) for the whole run, per B1 section, per B2 point and per B3 repetition; B1
+(`--section-quiet-wait-s`) and B2 (`--point-quiet-wait-s`) can wait for a quiet host before
+each section or point, and every suite takes `--threads N` (numba threads). A run
+interrupted by SIGINT/SIGTERM still writes its partial run directory.
+
+AWS: see `bench/aws/TEARDOWN.md` (bundle, user data, serial-console result channel,
+collection and teardown). `python bench/aws/dryrun_local.py --job selftest|smoke` rehearses
+the whole instance path in WSL2/Linux.
